{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794311759,"nonce":"c1kxUad/0QfKjFUWRMKoZZLV6Cg=","message":"P1 emit-only prototype (crates/ash/examples/air_wasm_emit.rs): optimized AIR -\u003e wasm function bodies, control flow structured from the dominator tree (loop per loop header, block per merge node, if per CondJump, br_table for Switch, phi copies on edges), cheap instructions as real wasm ops, every other instruction as a call to a typed helper import (one per kind/operand types/result type). Not modelled: exception handling (Trap/Throw are marker calls), phi copies on switch edges, the runtime semantics of the helpers, so it validates but does not run. Pocket Studio module (4038 functions, 264k instrs): emit 133-137 ms single-threaded (release, macOS), module 1.84 MB, 284 helpers, wasmparser validates ALL 4038 bodies (12 ms). 4 MB RayDE editor bytecode (9434 functions, 721k instrs): emit 363 ms, 4.8 MB, validates all 9434 bodies (31 ms). Scales linearly. A first run failed 1151 bodies: AIR Param also stands for non-argument registers (zeroed locals); the validator caught it. Caveats: real emission inlines field access, null checks, casts and allocation instead of one helper call, so expect several times this time and size, still well under a second; AIR itself costs 1.4 s here when computed cold (3.7 s for the editor bytecode), but the build normally reads it from the persistent AIR cache. Projected edit-loop build with a wasm backend: fixed stages 0.9-2.4 s + AIR cache read + emit (0.15-0.5 s) = roughly 1.5-3.3 s against 8.5-9 s today. Exit rule (coverage \u003e= 90%: 98.2%/100% with traps; emit + fixed stages \u003c 3 s): met on this input at the low end of the fixed-stage range, marginal if the fiber transform's 0.65 s and the larger link are added. Mac only; single input.","files":null}]}